"Form 10-Q for Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical INC filed 11/04/2026 (https://ir.ultragenyx.com/static-files/83f1b349-cb50-417a-8195-c53c514af23d)\nciteturn185search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; DTX301 is an AAV8 gene therapy product candidate, administered by a one-time IV infusion that is designed to deliver stable expression and activity of the OTC gene. ... In August 2026, we announced the FDA granted accelerated approval for GENGLYCOS, in adult and pediatric patients eight years and older with GSDIa.\n# Clinical Product Candidates\n\n## DTX301 (avalotcagene ontaparvovec) for the treatment of Ornithine Transcarbamylase, or OTC, deficiency\n\nDTX301 is an AAV8 gene therapy product candidate, administered by a one-time IV infusion that is designed to deliver stable expression and activity of the OTC gene. DTX301 is being developed for the treatment of patients with OTC deficiency, which is the most common urea cycle disorder, and there are approximately 10,000 patients in the developed world with OTC deficiency, of which we estimate approximately 80% are classified as late-onset, our target population. DTX301 has received Orphan Drug Designation in both the U.S. and in the EU and Fast Track Designation in the U.S.\n\n## UX143 (setrusumab) for the treatment of Osteogenesis Imperfecta, or OI\n\nUX143 is a fully human monoclonal antibody administered by IV that inhibits sclerostin, a protein that acts on a key bone-signaling pathway by inhibiting the activity of bone-forming cells and promoting bone resorption. UX143 is being developed for the treatment of OI, or brittle bone disease, which is caused by variants in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 genes, leading to either reduced or abnormal collagen and changes in bone metabolism. \n# Milestones and Upcoming Catalysts\n\n## GENGLYCOS for the treatment of GSDIa\n\nIn August 2026, we announced the FDA granted accelerated approval for GENGLYCOS, in adult and pediatric patients eight years and older with GSDIa. We also received a Rare Pediatric Disease Priority Review Voucher (PRV) upon this approval.\n\nIn October 2026, we entered into a definitive agreement to sell the PRV we received upon GENGLYCOS approval for a sales price of $210 million. The sale was completed on October 30, 2026.\n\n## FAYUVI for the treatment of MPS IIIA\n\nIn September 2026, we announced that the FDA granted standard full approval of FAYUVI for the treatment of pediatric patients with mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (MPS IIIA, Sanfilippo syndrome Type A). We also received a PRV upon this approval.\n\nIn October 2026, we entered into a definitive agreement to sell the PRV we received upon FAYUVI approval for a sales price of $203 million.\n\n## UX143 for the treatment of OI\n\nIn December 2025, we announced that the Phase 3 *Orbit* and *Cosmic* studies did not achieve their primary endpoint of reduction in annualized clinical fracture rate compared to placebo (*Orbit*) or bisphosphonates (*Cosmic*).\n\nIn October 2026, data with pre- and post-hoc analyses from *Orbit* and *Cosmic* were presented at the American Society for Bone and Mineral Research 2026 Annual Meeting showing a meaningful benefit on bone mineral density, fractures and other endpoints in the two Phase 3 studies. The Company will continue to engage regulatory agencies to determine the necessary data to support a regulatory filing and if there is a potential path forward for UX143.\n\n## DTX301 for the treatment of OTC deficiency\n\nThe Phase 3 *Enh3ance* placebo-controlled study showed substantial improvement in ammonia control and reduction need for scavenger medicines along with profound improvement in global impression scale. The study continues with patients in both treatment and placebo-crossover groups progressing through 64 weeks of follow-up to evaluate the second primary endpoint of the reduction in the use of ammonia scavengers and dietary management. Data are expected in the first half of 2027.\n\n26--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPipeline – OxThera (https://oxthera.com/pipeline/)\nciteturn185search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; OxThera’s current pipeline is built around exploring the therapeutic potential of Oxabact’s unique properties as an activated bimodal enteric biotherapy. ... Learn More on Oxabact’s Development Status\n\nOVERVIEW\n\nOxThera’s current pipeline is built around exploring the therapeutic potential of Oxabact’s unique properties as an activated bimodal enteric biotherapy.\n\nWhile we are pursuing this approach in Primary Hyperoxaluria as the lead indication, we believe there are also further diseases that could benefit from enteric biotherapy.\n\nOxabact®\n\nOxabact® is produced from well characterized anaerobic cell banks. The bacteria can exclusively metabolize oxalate\n\nOxabact® is believed to emit signal peptides that both activate and enhance the excretion of oxalate from plasma into the gut preventing the harmful accumulation of oxalate in plasma\n\nOur therapy can remove excess oxalate and may be able to deplete endogenous oxalate crystal deposits and thereby to preserve kidney function\n\nOxabact® has the potential to address\nthe systemic build-up of oxalate in organs like heart, bone and soft tissue\n\nIf developed successfully to market, Oxabact® will be the first orally delivered and therefore easy to use treatment for all patients suffering from Primary Hyperoxaluria\nOxabact® is not yet approved and not yet on the market.\n\nPre-clinical and clinical studies have demonstrated effect and safety. Long-term efficacy and safety now need to be confirmed through an ongoing Phase III clinical study and a further long-term follow-up study. No comparable product is yet on the market. It is OxThera’s intention to demonstrate to patients, clinicians, healthcare payers and regulators that our first-in-class approach, Oxabact®, provides clinical benefit for patients.\n\nOxazyme®\n\nOxazyme® is an orally delivered drug composed of recombinant oxalate decarboxylase, an enzyme cloned from B. subtilis, for treatment of Secondary hyperoxaluria. It is formulated to enzymatically degrade available dietary oxalate prior to its absorption in the intestine. This drug candidate is designed as a dietary intercept therapeutic for enteric and absorptive hyperoxalurias causing recurrent stone disease. The objective with Oxazyme® is to prevent formation of calcium-oxalate kidney stones.\n\nPre-clinical and early phase clinical studies have demonstrated effect and safety. Long-term efficacy and safety still need to be confirmed through additional Phase II and Phase III clinical studies to prove clinical benefit.\n\nLearn More on Oxabact’s Development Status\n\nClinical--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCorporate Presentation (https://ir.ultragenyx.com/static-files/ebde99f9-1891-4798-b0b2-f088bdb72131)\nciteturn185search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; August 23, 2026 ... DTX301\nKey clinical and regulatory catalysts\nPROGRAM​\nOBJECTIVE​\nANTICIPATED TIMING\nDTX401​\nGSDIa\nBLA accepted\nPDUFA decision date\nAugust 23, 2026\nUX111\nSanfilippo syndrome \nBLA accepted\nPDUFA decision date\nSeptember 19, 2026\nGTX-102​\nAngelman syndrome\nPhase 3 Aspire data\nPhase 2/3 Aurora enrollment completion\n2H-2026\n2H-2026\nUX701\nWilson disease\nStage 1 dose finding data (Cohorts 1-4)\n2026\nDTX301\nOTC deficiency\nPhase 3 ammonia analysis\nPhase 3 complete responder analysis\n1H-2027\nUX016\nGNE Myopathy\nPhase 1/2 IND clearance\nPhase 1/2 FPI\n2H-2026\nConfidential and Proprietary\n32--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCorporate Presentation (https://ir.ultragenyx.com/static-files/50d9ee53-214f-4b1b-ac72-29c86e484e73)\nciteturn185search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; # DTX301 for OTC: Safety / Next steps ... Data disclosed in press release on March 12, 2026\n\nCompany: Ultragenyx\n# DTX301 for OTC: Safety / Next steps\n\n- In Phase 3, DTX301 was well tolerated with acceptable safety profile, consistent with Phase 1/2\n- Most common treatment-emergent AE: mild to moderate hepatic reactions managed with steroids\n- One SAE of acute hepatitis assessed as treatment-related and resolved with steroids\n- Hyperammonemic crises were reduced relative to treated group: 5 placebo crises versus only 1 on DTX301\n  - One placebo patient died after presenting with grade 5 hyperammonemia with rapid decompensation with rising ammonia levels that were refractory to intensive medical intervention\n- No SAEs or AEs related to thrombotic microangiopathy, dorsal root ganglion toxicity, malignancies, or other complex immune reactions\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\n\n**Phase 3 study status / Next steps**\n\nPositive Phase 3 study continues to evaluate for complete responder rate\n\nData expected in 1H-2027\n\n</visual_element>\n\n---\n\nPage number: 22  \nConfidential and Proprietary\n\nData disclosed in press release on March 12, 2026\n\nCompany: Ultragenyx--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2025 Impact Report (https://ir.ultragenyx.com/static-files/d377fe9a-01a3-4549-a540-7697893d907c)\nciteturn185search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; | **DTX301** | AAV8-OTC gene therapy | Magenta pipeline bar: Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) | Magenta bar continues | Magenta bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | IV Infusion | **~10,000** | ... © 2026 Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc.\n- **Purple:** Bone/ Endo\n\n## Clinical Pipeline\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n\n| Candidate | Description | Phase 1 | Phase 2 | Phase 3 | Route of Admin | Prevalence¹ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **GTX-102** | ASO activating paternal expression of UBE3A | Green pipeline bar: Angelman Syndrome (AS) | Green bar continues | Green bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | Intrathecal (IT) Infusion | **~60,000** |\n| **UX016²** | Small molecule prodrug | Green pipeline arrow: GNE Myopathy; arrow located in Phase 1 | — | — | Oral substrate replacement | **~10,000** |\n| **UX111** | AAV9 SGSH gene therapy | Magenta pipeline bar: Sanfilippo Syndrome (MPS IIIA) | Magenta bar continues | Magenta bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | Intravenous (IV) Infusion | **~3,000 - 5,000** |\n| **DTX401** | AAV8-G6Pase gene therapy | Magenta pipeline bar: Glycogen Storage Disease Type Ia (GSDIa) | Magenta bar continues | Magenta bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | IV Infusion | **~6,000** |\n| **DTX301** | AAV8-OTC gene therapy | Magenta pipeline bar: Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) | Magenta bar continues | Magenta bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | IV Infusion | **~10,000** |\n| **UX701** | AAV9-ATP7B gene therapy | Magenta pipeline bar: Wilson Disease (WD) | Magenta bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 2 | — | IV Infusion | **~50,000** |\n| **UX143 (setrusumab)³** | Anti-Sclerostin monoclonal antibody | Gray pipeline bar: Osteogenesis Imperfecta (OI) | Gray bar continues | Gray bar continues to arrow endpoint in Phase 3 | IV Infusion | **~60,000** |\n\n</table>\n\n*As of May 2026*\n\nFor the latest information on our pipeline, please visit our website.\n\n### Footnotes\n\n1. Prevalence in commercially accessible geographies\n2. Externally funded by a venture philanthropy agreement through clinical proof-of-concept\n3. As of the publication date of this report, based on the Company's analysis of the data across the two global Phase 3 studies, the Company believes there is a basis to engage regulatory agencies to determine if there is a path forward for the program.\n\n© 2026 Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc.\n\nPage 14--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCorporate Presentation (https://ultragenyx.gcs-web.com/static-files/50d9ee53-214f-4b1b-ac72-29c86e484e73)\nciteturn185search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; | Change in OTCD symptoms | DTX301 | n=14 | 43% | 21% | 14% | 22% | 64% | ... Data disclosed in press release on March 12, 2026\n\nUltragenyx\n# DTX301 for OTC: At Week 24 patients treated with DTX301 showed improvements in feeling and functioning compared to placebo\n\n## At Week 24, 86% of DTX301 patients showed clinically important improvement in OTCD vs 13% placebo patients\n\n### Week 24 Patient Global Impression of Change (PGIC) Score\n\nChart: Patient Global Impression of Change (PGIC) Score at Week 24.\n\nVertical axis: Patient Global Impression of Change (PGIC) Score.\n\nHorizontal axis: Three outcome categories, each comparing DTX301-treated patients with placebo-treated patients:\n1. Overall change in OTCD\n2. Change in OTCD symptoms\n3. Change in impact of OTCD on daily life\n\nLegend:\n- Dark blue: Much Improved\n- Light blue: Moderately Improved\n- Light purple: Minimally Improved\n- Light gray: No change\n\n### Chart data\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n\n| Outcome category | Treatment group | Sample size | Much Improved | Moderately Improved | Minimally Improved | No change | Clinically important improvement |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Overall change in OTCD | DTX301 | n=7 | 71% | 15% | 0% | 14% | 86% |\n| Overall change in OTCD | Placebo | n=8 | 0% | 13% | 50% | 37% | 13% |\n| Change in OTCD symptoms | DTX301 | n=14 | 43% | 21% | 14% | 22% | 64% |\n| Change in OTCD symptoms | Placebo | n=16 | 0% | 19% | 25% | 56% | 19% |\n| Change in impact of OTCD on daily life | DTX301 | n=14 | 43% | 21% | 14% | 22% | 64% |\n| Change in impact of OTCD on daily life | Placebo | n=16 | 0% | 19% | 12% | 69% | 19% |\n\n</table>\n\nClinically important change is moderate improvement (+2) or much improved change (+3).\n\n## Change from baseline in Week 24 PGIC scores\n\n### DTX301 treated patients\n- 86% demonstrated clinically important change in overall OTCD, with 71% much improved.\n\n### Placebo treated patients\n- 87% did not demonstrate clinically important changes in overall OTCD.\n- Majority reported “no change” on impact to disease, symptoms, or daily life.\n\n---\n\nSlide number: 21\n\nConfidential and Proprietary\n\nData disclosed in press release on March 12, 2026\n\nUltragenyx--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpruce Biosciences Reports Third Quarter 2026 Financial Results and Provides Corporate Updates (https://investors.sprucebio.com/node/9346/pdf)\nciteturn185search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; *Biologics License Application (“BLA”) Submission for Tralesinidase Alfa Enzyme Replacement Therapy (“TA-ERT”) for the Treatment of MPS IIIB Remains on Track for December 2026*\n# Spruce Biosciences Reports Third Quarter 2026 Financial Results and Provides Corporate Updates\n\n**November 9, 2026**\n\n*Biologics License Application (“BLA”) Submission for Tralesinidase Alfa Enzyme Replacement Therapy (“TA-ERT”) for the Treatment of MPS IIIB Remains on Track for December 2026*\n\n*Amended Avenue Loan Agreement to Increase Tranche 2 Availability to $15 Million and Extend Interest-Only Period*\n\n*Announced Separation of CRHX-101 (Formerly SPR-202) Program into Adrenyx Biosciences*\n\nSOUTH SAN FRANCISCO, Calif.--(BUSINESS WIRE)--Nov. 9, 2026-- Spruce Biosciences, Inc. (Nasdaq: SPRB), a late-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on developing and commercializing novel therapies for neurological disorders with significant unmet medical need, today reported financial results for the third quarter ended September 30, 2026, and provided corporate updates.\n\n“We are executing with discipline as we complete the final steps toward our planned BLA submission for TA-ERT in December, which we believe will be a defining milestone for Spruce and for the children and families living with MPS IIIB,” said Javier Szwarcberg, M.D., M.P.H., Chief Executive Officer of Spruce Biosciences. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClinical Study of DTX301 AAV-Mediated Gene Transfer for Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) Deficiency | Clinical Research Trial Listing (https://www.centerwatch.com/clinical-trials/listings/NCT05345171/clinical-study-of-dtx301-aav-mediated-gene-transfer-for-ornithine-transcarbamylaseotc-deficiency)\nciteturn185search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Clinical Study of DTX301 AAV-Mediated Gene Transfer for Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) Deficiency\n\nLast updated: September 8, 2026\n\nSponsor: Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc\n\n# Clinical Study of DTX301 AAV-Mediated Gene Transfer for Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) Deficiency\n\nLast updated: September 8, 2026\n\nSponsor: Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc\n\nOverall Status: Active - Not Recruiting\n\n#### Phase\n\n3\n\n#### Condition\n\nN/A\n\n#### Treatment\n\nDTX301\n\nPlacebo\n\nPlacebo for oral corticosteroids\n\n#### Clinical Study ID\n\nNCT05345171\n\nDTX301-CL301\n\n2024-514337-38-00\n\n2020-003384-25\n\n  * Ages > 12\n  * All Genders\n\n### Study Summary\n\nThe primary objective is to evaluate the efficacy of DTX301 on the improvement of ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) function by maintaining safe plasma ammonia levels.\n\n## Eligibility Criteria\n\n#### Inclusion\n\nKey Inclusion Criteria:\n\n  * Confirmed clinical diagnosis of late-onset OTC deficiency with historicaldocumentation by enzymatic (ie, liver biopsy), biochemical (ie, hyperammonemia inthe presence of elevated plasma glutamine, low citrulline, and elevated spot urineorotic acid), or molecular testing (ie, OTC analysis)\n\n  * Free from symptomatic hyperammonemia and has not required emergent activeintervention for hyperammonemia within 4 weeks before screening/baseline\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSEC Filing—Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc. (https://ir.ultragenyx.com/node/19136/html)\nciteturn185search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: today; UX016 novel prodrug for sialic acid used as a substrate replacement therapy for the treatment of GNE myopathy: The FDA cleared the Investigational New Drug (IND) application for UX016 and an externally funded Phase 1/2 study is expected to begin in the second half of 2026. ... DTX301 (avalotcagene ontaparvovec) AAV8 gene therapy for the treatment of Ornithine Transcarbamylase, or OTC, deficiency: The Phase 3 Enh3ance study continues with patients in both treatment and cross-over groups progressing through 64 weeks of follow-up.\n \n\n•\n\nUX016 novel prodrug for sialic acid used as a substrate replacement therapy for the treatment of GNE myopathy: The FDA cleared the Investigational New Drug (IND) application for UX016 and an externally funded Phase 1/2 study is expected to begin in the second half of 2026.\n\n•\n\nDTX301 (avalotcagene ontaparvovec) AAV8 gene therapy for the treatment of Ornithine Transcarbamylase, or OTC, deficiency: The Phase 3 Enh3ance study continues with patients in both treatment and cross-over groups progressing through 64 weeks of follow-up. Data from the second primary endpoint, which evaluates reduction in treatment burden, including use of ammonia scavengers and dietary management, are expected in the first half of 2027.\n\n \n\n \n\nSummary of Second Quarter 2026 Financial Results\n\nSelected Financial Data (dollars in millions, except per share amounts), (unaudited)\n\n \n\n |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  | \n   | Three Months Ended June 30,  |    | Six Months Ended June 30,  |  \n   | 2026  |    |    | 2025  |    | 2026  |    |    | 2025  |  \nTotal revenues  | $  | 214  |    |    | $  | 167  |    | $  | 350  |    |    | $  | 306  |  \nOperating expenses:  |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |  \nCost of sales  |    | 34  |    |    |    | 23  |    |    | 64  |    |    |    | 52  |  \nResearch and development  |    | 167  |    |    |    | 165  |    |    | 354  |    |    |    | 331  |  \nSelling, general and administrative  |    | 88  |    |    |    | 87  |    |    | 176  |    |    |    | 174  |  \nTotal operating expenses  |    | 289  |    |    |    | 275  |    |    | 594  |    |    |    | 557  |  \nNet loss  | $  | (92  | )  |    | $  | (115  | )  | $  | (277  | )  |    | $  | (266  | )\nNet loss per share, basic and diluted  | $  | (0.90  | )  |    | $  | (1.17  | )  | $  | (2.73  | )  |    | $  | (2.73  | )\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClinical – OxThera (https://oxthera.com/clinical/)\nciteturn185search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Initial Development Path ... Oxabact® OC5 product has been used in all clinical trial since late 2013. ... In May 2014, OxThera initiated a study in PH patients with ESKD on dialysis. ... An interim analysis of the 8 patients reaching the 24 month treatment milestone was recently published (Hoppe B. et al., NDT, 2020) and demonstrated a clinically relevant and statistically significant reduction of total plasma oxalate (Pox) of about 40% across 24 months of treatment together with a stabilisation of free (soluble) Pox.\n\nCLINICAL STUDIES\n\nClinical studies with Oxabact® have been performed with both earlier formulations and with the high-dose Oxabact® OC5 product candidate. Overall, Oxabact® has been tested and was well tolerated in over 100 patients with primary Hyperoxaluria.\n\nInitial Development Path\n\nEarlier studies with Oxabact® showed a substantial decrease in urinary oxalate excretion in subjects with PH. Several subsequent trials with this formulation failed to reach the primary endpoint, most likely because the patient population enrolled was too heterogeneous and the studies were too short. It was also determined that the formulation used in these studies had a low viable cell count and slow recovery rate of the bacteria from freeze-dried powder, once released in the intestinal tract. Drug manufacturing changes resulted in an improved and highly concentrated Oxabact® OC5 product. OC5 has a higher viable cell count and faster recovery from the freeze-dried state than its predecessors. Several other improvements in the manufacturing process were also made.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUltragenyx Pharmaceutical (RARE) Transcript: UBS Global Biopharma Conference 2026 (https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/rare/transcripts/801016-ubs-global-biopharma-conference-2026/)\nciteturn185search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; Crawled: today; CEO, Ultragenyx ... DTX301.\n\nYeah. We can go over a few more minutes. We have to talk about DTX401 for OTC. You already reported.\n\nEmil Kakkis\n\nCEO, Ultragenyx\n\nDTX301.\n\nJoon Lee\n\nBiotech Analyst, UBS\n\nOh, sorry. Oh, 401's approved.\n\nEmil Kakkis\n\nCEO, Ultragenyx\n\nYes.\n\nJoon Lee\n\nBiotech Analyst, UBS\n\nYeah, sorry. DTX301 for OTC. You already reported very strong data from the blind portion of data, study phase III Enh3ance trial. What are you trying to achieve from the open label portion?\n\nEmil Kakkis\n\nCEO, Ultragenyx\n\nYeah, so the blinded portion is really the important one. That showed excellent ammonia control, but more importantly than just ammonia control.\n\nJoon Lee\n\nBiotech Analyst, UBS\n\nYeah.\n\nEmil Kakkis\n\nCEO, Ultragenyx\n\nIt talked about their brains getting clearer. The PGI-C, the Global Impression scales scores, showed 89% plus two, plus three. That means much better or very much better. 75% were plus three, that is very much better, versus 0% placebo. Even though these patients were stably on meds for OTC, standard of care, they felt much better. That is what the difference in having a gene therapy is that they get the actual enzyme and they control ammonia tightly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOur Clinical Trial Results—Ultragenyx (https://www.ultragenyx.com/our-research/our-clinical-trial-results/)\ncite